      \field{abstract}{GaInAs lattice matched to InP was grown by molecular-beam epitaxy over a temperature range of 100--450\,{$^\circ$}C and characterized by x-ray diffraction, resistivity, and secondary ion mass spectroscopy. X-ray diffraction analysis indicated the incorporation of excess As for samples grown below 250\,{$^\circ$}C. As-grown GaInAs was n-type, with electrical concentration increasing with decreasing growth temperature, ranging from 5\texttimes 1014 cm-3 (450\,{$^\circ$}C) to 1.8\texttimes 1017 (150\,{$^\circ$}C). Secondary ion mass spectroscopy indicated that this behavior was not due to the incorporation of background n-type dopants. Be- and Si-doped GaInAs at 5\texttimes 1018 cm-3 showed full electrical activation for growth temperatures down to 260\,{$^\circ$}C, with only partial activation for Si and no activation for Be for growth temperatures below 260\,{$^\circ$}C.}
