      \field{labelnamesource}{author}
      \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
      \field{abstract}{This chapter deals with semiconductors and their surface chemistry. Semiconductor materials, including the group IV elements Si and Ge, play a dominant role in much of modern technology. It begins by reviewing the surface structure of the most commonly studied crystal faces of these materials. This includes the (100) and (111) crystal faces of silicon and germanium. Following this, it briefly discusses the oxidation of both silicon and germanium, and reviews the passivation of silicon and germanium surfaces. In addition, it explores the reactivity of Si, Ge, and to a lesser extent GaAs and InP, toward various organic molecules that can be used to functionalize the surface and impart new properties to the semiconductor. The study of organic functionalization of semiconductors, in which organic molecules are adsorbed onto the surface in vacuum, is providing new insight into the reactivity of the reconstructed semiconductor surfaces. Several common themes have emerged in the understanding of the reactivity of both silicon and germanium based on these studies. Furthermore, the organic functionalization studies have indicated that multiple reaction products can form even for simple systems. Kinetic and thermodynamic influences must be considered in any analysis of the product distribution. Finally, increased level of understanding of the behavior of silicon and germanium surfaces toward organic reagents is banked upon to enable the next generation of studies, in which organic functionalization can be achieved selectively and controllably for a variety of new applications.}
